# ── Next.js (this file → .env.local) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Written for you by `npx convex dev`. On Vercel it is injected by the build
# command (`convex deploy -y --cmd-url-env-var-name NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL`), so
# do NOT set it in the Vercel dashboard.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=
# Optional. The deployment's HTTP-actions address (https://<deployment>.convex.site).
# Leave it unset and it is derived from NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL (.convex.cloud
# becomes .convex.site). Set it only when that derivation is wrong for this
# same deployment. A value that names a different deployment is ignored, so a
# dev address left in Vercel cannot send OAuth discovery and token refresh at
# an old backend.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_SITE_URL=
# Optional. The app's public origin: https, no path, no query, no credentials.
# Set it (in Vercel, for Production) once the domain serves the app, and the
# "Connect your Claude" prompt shows <this>/ingest/v1 instead of the
# deployment's *.convex.site address. The app passes POST /ingest/v1,
# GET /standup/v1/open and POST /standup/v1/tasks through to the deployment
# whether or not this is set (docs/ingest.md, "The endpoint's two addresses").
# Unset, the prompt shows the deployment's address, as before.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OS_URL=https://os.example.com

# ── Vercel (dashboard, not this file) ────────────────────────────────────────
# CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY   set twice: the production key scoped to Production only,
#                     and a preview key scoped to Preview only. One key for
#                     both would either break every preview build or let a
#                     pull request deploy to production.

# ── Convex (dashboard or `npx convex env set`, not this file) ────────────────
# Everything below is read inside Convex functions, so it lives in the Convex
# deployment's environment. Never put these in Vercel or commit them.
#
# OWNER_EMAILS                 Comma-separated Google accounts allowed in.
#                              Unset means nobody can use the app.
# AUTH_GOOGLE_ID               Google OAuth client id.
# AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET           Google OAuth client secret.
# SITE_URL                     Where the app is served, e.g. https://os.example.com
#                              (http://localhost:3100 in dev).
# JWT_PRIVATE_KEY, JWKS        Session signing keys. Generate and set with
#                              `node scripts/generate-auth-keys.mjs --set`.
# INGEST_TOKEN_CFO_WORKBOOK    Bearer token for POST /ingest/v1 as the
#                              cfo_workbook source. 32 or more letters, digits,
#                              "-" or "_": `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# INGEST_TOKEN_VISIBLE         Bearer token for POST /ingest/v1 as the visible
#                              source (the investor board). `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# INGEST_TOKEN_KZ_CRM          The same for the kz_crm source (Kazakhstan deals).
#                              A different value: two sources sharing a token
#                              are both refused.
# MERCURY_API_TOKEN            A Mercury Read Only API token (Settings → API
#                              tokens in Mercury). The daily sync reads cash and
#                              monthly money in and out with it.
# POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY     A PostHog personal API key with the Query Read
#                              scope, limited to the Startup Insights project and
#                              Globex's app project. Not the project key the
#                              site captures with.
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID           Startup Insights' project number.
# POSTHOG_GLOBEX_PROJECT_ID    Globex's app (app.globex.example.com) project number,
#                              read by the posthog_globex source with the same
#                              key. Unset, that source says "not configured".
# POSTHOG_HOST                 Optional: https://eu.posthog.com for EU Cloud.
#                              Defaults to https://us.posthog.com.
# VERCEL_API_TOKEN             A Vercel access token scoped to the startup team.
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID               The team's id (team_…).
# VERCEL_PROJECT_ID            The startup-insights project's id (prj_…).
# VERCEL_WEB_ANALYTICS         Optional: "on" to also read weekly page views.
#                              Leave unset unless the plan includes the Web
#                              Analytics API; when on, a refusal fails the run.
# VERTICAL_HEALTH_URL             https://<vertical deployment>.convex.site/os/health
# VERTICAL_HEALTH_SECRET          The same value as OS_HEALTH_SECRET on the Vertical
#                              Startup deployment. 32 or more characters:
#                              `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# GSC_CLIENT_EMAIL             The Search Console service account's email
#                              (client_email in its JSON key file). Add this
#                              address to the property as a Restricted user.
# GSC_PRIVATE_KEY              The private_key field of that JSON file, whole.
#                              A secret: set it here and delete the file.
# GSC_SITE_URL                 The property as Search Console names it:
#                              sc-domain:example.com, or https://example.com/
# APOLLO_API_KEY               An Apollo API key allowed to call "Search for
#                              Sequences". Apollo's reference says that needs a
#                              master key; try a key scoped to that endpoint first.
#
# Work tools (Phase 8). All read-only; set only the ones you use.
# GOOGLE_WORK_CLIENT_ID        A second Google OAuth client, for "Connect Google"
# GOOGLE_WORK_CLIENT_SECRET    (Gmail headers and Calendar events). Not the
#                              sign-in client: type Web application, consent
#                              screen Internal, redirect URI
#                              https://<deployment>.convex.site/google/callback.
# TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY         Seals the stored Google grant. 32 random bytes,
#                              base64: `openssl rand -base64 32`. Changing or
#                              losing it only means connecting Google again.
# LINEAR_API_KEY               A Linear personal API key (read-only if offered).
# FIREFLIES_API_KEY            A Fireflies API key.
#
# Telegram (Settings → Telegram). Optional: unset, no message is ever sent.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN           The token @BotFather gives a new bot. It is read
#                              from here only: never stored in a table, shown
#                              on a page or written to a log. The group each
#                              company's messages go to is picked on the page.
#                              SITE_URL above gives a message its board link.
#
# A bot asks for a fix (`dev_request`, docs/assistant.md "Asking for a fix").
# Optional: unset, requests wait in Startup OS and nothing is sent to GitHub.
# GITHUB_ISSUES_TOKEN          A fine-grained personal access token, resource
#                              owner your-org, repository your-org/your-os
#                              only: Issues read and write (and Pull requests
#                              read, so a merged fix counts as shipped). Set in
#                              the production deployment. Read inside the
#                              filing and checking actions only: never stored,
#                              shown or logged.
#
# Waking an external bot (docs/assistant.md, "Waking an external bot").
# Optional: unset URL, nothing is sent and nothing fails.
# BOT_EVENTS_WEBHOOK_URL       https address that receives task.assigned,
#                              handoff.assigned and proposal.decided, for
#                              every bot: the receiver routes on `agent`.
#                              Never a private, loopback or link-local
#                              address; http://localhost only on a local
#                              development deployment. Never committed.
# BOT_EVENTS_WEBHOOK_SECRET    The key each request is signed with
#                              (X-Startup-Signature, HMAC-SHA256 of
#                              "timestamp.body"); at least 32 characters,
#                              `openssl rand -hex 32`. Unset: nothing is sent.
#                              Never logged or stored.
# BOT_EVENTS_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREVIOUS
#                              Optional, while rotating: the old key, sent as
#                              X-Startup-Signature-Previous. Unset it once the
#                              receiver has the new one.
#
# The push script (`pnpm push:retention`) reads from an untracked .env.push
# beside package.json, on the machine that runs it, not from Convex:
# STARTUP_OS_INGEST_URL          https://os.example.com/ingest/v1, or the deployment's
#                              own https://<deployment>.convex.site/ingest/v1
# INGEST_TOKEN_CFO_WORKBOOK    The same token as above.
#
# The client register (`pnpm push:customers`, the one push that carries client
# names) has its own token, set in both places like the workbook's:
# INGEST_TOKEN_CLIENT_REGISTER Bearer token for POST /ingest/v1 as the
#                              client_register source. A different value from
#                              the workbook's: one token for both authenticates
#                              neither. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# Fed by a Claude connector run instead of an API key (docs/ingest.md,
# "Feeding the OS from Claude connectors"). Each is the bearer token for
# POST /ingest/v1 as that source: set it in Convex AND in .env.push on the
# machine that runs `pnpm push:envelope`, and leave the source's own API
# variables above unset (while they are set the OS reads the source itself and
# refuses the push). A different value each, `openssl rand -hex 32`: two
# sources sharing a token are both refused.
# INGEST_TOKEN_POSTHOG         Startup Insights' weekly numbers (snapshots).
# INGEST_TOKEN_MERCURY         US cash and monthly money in and out (snapshots).
# INGEST_TOKEN_APOLLO          Outbound email totals to date (snapshots).
# INGEST_TOKEN_GMAIL           Threads waiting on a reply (records).
# INGEST_TOKEN_GOOGLE_CALENDAR Meetings (records).
# INGEST_TOKEN_LINEAR          The issue board (records).
# INGEST_TOKEN_FIREFLIES       Meeting notes (records).
#
# Engineering signals from GitHub (docs/setup.md, "GitHub"): a script
# on the founder's machine, `pnpm gather:github`, counts pull requests and build
# results through the GitHub CLI and pushes them. Set this in Convex AND in
# .env.push on the machine that runs it; its own value, `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# This script uses no GitHub token: `gh` holds its own sign-in (`gh auth
# login`), and the script never reads or prints it. (The one GitHub token in
# Convex is GITHUB_ISSUES_TOKEN below, for the bots' requests, Issues only.)
# INGEST_TOKEN_GITHUB          Pull requests by week, open and waiting ones, and
#                              each repository's main-branch build (snapshots,
#                              and records of kind "repo"). Never code, a title
#                              or a person.
#
# The daily standup's tasks (docs/ingest.md, "Standup tasks"): a Claude session
# reads the standup's summary in Fireflies and adds the missing tasks to the
# board's Backlog. Not POST /ingest/v1: it is the bearer token for
# GET /standup/v1/open and POST /standup/v1/tasks, and the only one that can
# READ company data (the titles of the board's cards and of Linear's open
# issues). Set it in Convex AND in .env.push on the machine that runs
# `pnpm standup:open` and `pnpm standup:push`; its own value,
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. Unset, both routes answer 503.
# INGEST_TOKEN_STANDUP         Tasks from the daily standup.
#
# Vertical Startup's AI agents (the Agents page; docs/ingest.md, "Vertical Startup's
# agents"). Vertical Startup's daily cron pushes the agents' totals; the OS sends
# back configuration only, signed. Each its own value, `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# INGEST_TOKEN_VERTICAL_AGENTS    Bearer token for POST /ingest/v1 as the
#                              vertical_agents source: set it here and as Vertical
#                              Startup's own variable for its cron.
# VERTICAL_STARTUP_URL              Vertical Startup's https origin, nothing after it
#                              (https://vertical.example.com). Configs are posted
#                              to <it>/api/admin/agent-config.
# VERTICAL_AGENT_CONFIG_SECRET    The HMAC-SHA256 key that signs each config, the
#                              same value on Vertical Startup's side. 32 or more
#                              characters. Never stored in a table or shown.
